            <ArticleTitle>&quot;Naked&quot; Lithium Cation: Strongly Activated Metal Cations Facilitated by Carborane Anions.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1931-1935</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/acs.joc.6b02677</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Experimental and spectroscopic studies revealed unprecedented reactivity of a &quot;naked&quot; lithium cation with very weakly coordinating anions, including carborane anions. The superactivated lithium cation has greatly enhanced Lewis acidic character and mediates various organic reactions such as carbonyl-ene reaction, NBS-bromination of unactivated aromatics, and Friedel-Crafts alkylation, which are not promoted by conventional lithium salts. Chemical robustness of the counteranion also plays an important role in the chemistry of the strongly activated lithium cation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
